e  to  well i i don't know but i doubt it  um  you know i think that  it was interesting to me when they when  they held their news conference and they  showed this is the rq 170 sentinel  uh which previously had sort of been  known to reporters as the beast of  kandahar because  they would just get glimpses of it from  time to time  a sort of batwing-shaped  drone but when that was put on display  in iran one of the notable things about  it was that they didn't show you the  landing gear  and uh they had a flag or something  draped across the front  um and and i have talked to people who  know more about these things than i who  who say that you know that suggests to  them that that aircraft came in for a  pretty hard landing  and  it's unlikely that it was intact  when they found it  it looked like a strange  beast uh actually when they showed it at  their news conference  uh but but i don't know the answer to  that question i mean that was a that's a  classified uh it was a classified  aircraft  uh before the iranians uh got it and but  uh i've also been told and i think it  makes more sense to me than anything  else that wasn't a case of the iranians  taking it over and bringing it in as  they said either i think that it lost  link  it  you know lost the signal  and  went and it went down and probably had a  hard landing  i have answered every question any  additional comment and question  feel free to  chime in  there's one more please richard douglas  um  rick you know you've written your  previous book about the v22 which i  think is more of a characteristic  program about the aerospace and defense  industry it's more incremental this  really is as you say a revolution it's a  tremendous story and the v22 great story  too but more incremental there are very  few opportunities to observe a  revolution uh as it happens in the  aerospace or defense industry do you see  anything else on the in the future on  the horizon that could  approximate something of this m